# Class: obo:ND_0000067 (polyradiculoneuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000067 "Diseases characterized by injury or dysfunction involving multiple peripheral nerves and nerve roots. The process may primarily affect myelin or nerve axons. Two of the more common demyelinating forms are acute inflammatory polyradiculopathy ( GUILLAIN-BARRE SYNDROME) and POLYRADICULONEUROPATHY, CHRONIC INFLAMMATORY DEMYELINATING. Polyradiculoneuritis refers to inflammation of multiple peripheral nerves and spinal nerve roots (MeSH).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000067 "Polyradiculoneuropathy describes a condition in which polyneuropathy and polyradiculopathy occur together. An example is Guillain-Barré syndrome.

Treatment with a single course of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) infusions has been demonstrated to be a potentially effective treatment (reported to have caused prolonged remission in a case associated with systemic lupus (Systemic lupus erythematosus)).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000067 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000067 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyradiculoneuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000067 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Polyradiculoneuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000067 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12553")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000067 "See the comment for 'demyelinating autoimmune disease'.  Perhaps this class should be relocated under the class 'demyelinating autoimmune disease' since inflamation is an autoimmune response.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000067 "polyradiculoneuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000067 obo:ND_0000024)

# Class: obo:ND_0000068 (alpha synucleinopathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000068 "Alpha-synucleinopathy is a neurological disease ................. that results in the degeneration of populations of nerve cells that develop Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites as a result of filamentous inclusions made of the protein alpha-synuclein.

*** NEEDS REVIEW ***")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000068 "[PROPOSED] initially affects the medulla oblongata and progresses to more rostral brain areas in a hierarchical sequence (\"Braak hypothesis\"). Predominant involvement of the amygdala is also described.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000068 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000068 "alpha-synucleinopathies")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000068 "alpha-synucleinopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000068 "α-synucleinopathies")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000068 "α-synucleinopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000068 "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18362284")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000068 "Besides Parkinson's disease, the filamentous inclusions of two additional neurodegenerative diseases, namely, dementia with Lewy bodies and multiple system atrophy, have also been found to be made of alpha-synuclein. Recombinant alpha-synuclein has been shown to assemble into filaments with similar morphologies to those found in the human diseases and with a cross-beta fiber diffraction pattern. The new work has established the alpha-synucleinopathies as a major class of neurodegenerative disease.

From: http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11193145")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000068 "def [proposed] is hypothetical")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000068 "alpha synucleinopathy")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000068 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000113 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000113 obo:ND_0005509) ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006037)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000068 obo:ND_0000113)

# Class: obo:ND_0000069 (Guillain-Barre syndrome)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000069 "Guillain–Barré syndrome (GBS), sometimes called Landry's paralysis, is an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP), a disorder affecting the peripheral nervous system. Ascending paralysis, weakness beginning in the feet and hands and migrating towards the trunk, is the most typical symptom. It can cause life-threatening complications, particularly if the breathing muscles are affected or if there is dysfunction of the autonomic nervous system. The disease is usually triggered by an acute infection. Guillain–Barré syndrome is a form of peripheral neuropathy.

The diagnosis is usually made by nerve conduction studies. With prompt treatment by intravenous immunoglobulins or plasmapheresis, together with supportive care, the majority will recover completely. Guillain–Barré syndrome is rare, at 1–2 cases per 100,000 people annually, but is one of the leading causes of acute non-trauma-related paralysis in the world. The syndrome is named after the French physicians Georges Guillain and Jean Alexandre Barré, who described it in 1916.

Six different subtypes of Guillain–Barré syndrome exist.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000069 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000069 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillain%E2%80%93Barr%C3%A9_syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000069 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12554")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000069 "Add the other 5 subtypes.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000069 "Guillain-Barre syndrome")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000069 obo:ND_0000067)

# Class: obo:ND_0000070 (Miller-Fisher syndrome)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000070 "Miller Fisher syndrome (MFS) is a rare variant of GBS and manifests as a descending paralysis, proceeding in the reverse order of the more common form of GBS. It usually affects the eye muscles first and presents with the triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia. Anti-GQ1b antibodies are present in 90% of cases.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000070 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000070 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillain%E2%80%93Barr%C3%A9_syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000070 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12555")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000070 "Miller-Fisher syndrome")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000070 obo:ND_0000069)

# Class: obo:ND_0000071 (hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000071 "Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy (HSAN) or hereditary sensory neuropathy (HSN) is a condition used to describe any of the types of this disease which inhibit sensation.

Five different clinical entities have been described under hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies – all characterized by progressive loss of function that predominantly affects the peripheral sensory nerves.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000071 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000071 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereditary_sensory_and_autonomic_neuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000071 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12556")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000071 "Add 'type 3 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy' as a subclass of 'hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy'.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000071 "hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000071 obo:ND_0000067)

# Class: obo:ND_0000072 (type 1 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000072 "Type 1 is the most common of the hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies (HSAN). Current names are: hereditary sensory neuropathy type I (HSN I), hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type I (HSAN I). Historical names include: Hereditary sensory radicular neuropathy, ulcero-mutilating neuropathy, thevenard syndrome, familial trophoneurosis, mal perforant du pied, and familial syringomyelia. Sub-type 1C is also currently known as Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 2B syndrome (HMSN 2B).

Type 1 is transmitted as autosomal dominant trait and is characterized by a sensory deficit in the distal portion of the lower extremities, chronic perforating ulcerations of the feet and progressive destruction of underlying bones. Sweating abnormalities occur but other autonomic features are not found in this type. Symptoms appear in late childhood on early adolescence with trophic ulcers as pain sensation is affected more. Many patients have accompanying nerve deafness and atrophy of the peroneal muscles. Histopathologic examination reveals a marked reduction in the number of unmyelinated fibers. Motor nerve conduction velocities are normal, but the sensory nerve action potentials are absent.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000072 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "familial syringomyelia")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "familial trophoneurosis")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "hereditary sensory radicular neuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "mal perforant du pied")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "thevenard syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000072 "ulcero-mutilating neuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000072 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereditary_sensory_and_autonomic_neuropathy#Type_1")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000072 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12557")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000072 "type 1 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000072 obo:ND_0000071)

# Class: obo:ND_0000073 (type 2 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000073 "Type 2, congenital sensory neuropathy (also historically known as Morvan's disease), is characterized by onset of symptoms in early infancy or childhood. Upper & lower extremities are affected with chronic ulcerations and multiple injuries to fingers and feet. Pain sensation is affected predominantly and deep tendon reflexes are reduced. Autoamputation of the distal phalanges is common and so is neuropathic joint degeneration. The NCV shows reduced or absent sensory nerve action potentials and nerve biopsy shows total loss of myelinated fibers and reduced numbers of unmyelinated fibers. It is inherited as an autosomal recessive condition.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000073 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000073 "Morvan's disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000073 "congenital sensory neuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000073 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereditary_sensory_and_autonomic_neuropathy#Type_2.2C_Congenital_sensory_neuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000073 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12558")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000073 "type 2 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000073 obo:ND_0000071)

# Class: obo:ND_0000074 (type 4 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000074 "Type 4, congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis (CIPA), is an autosomal recessive condition and affected infants present with episodes of hyperthermia unrelated to environmental temperature, anhidrosis and insensitivity to pain. Palmar skin is thickened and charcot joints are commonly present. NCV shows motor and sensory nerve action potentials to be normal. The histopathology of peripheral nerve biopsy reveals absent small unmyelinated fibers and mitochondria are abnormally enlarged.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000074 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000074 "CIPA")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000074 "congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000074 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereditary_sensory_and_autonomic_neuropathy#Type_4.2C_Congenital_insensitivity_to_pain_with_anhidrosis")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000074 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12559")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000074 "type 4 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000074 obo:ND_0000071)

# Class: obo:ND_0000075 (type 5 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000075 "Type 5, congenital insensitivity to pain with partial anhidrosis, also manifests with congenital insensitivity to pain & anhidrosis. There is a selective absence of small myelinated fibers differentiating it from Type IV (CIPA).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000075 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000075 "congenital insensitivity to pain with partial anhidrosis")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000075 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereditary_sensory_and_autonomic_neuropathy#Type_5.2C_Congenital_insensitivity_to_pain_with_partial_anhidrosis")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000075 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12560")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000075 "type 5 hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000075 obo:ND_0000071)

# Class: obo:ND_0000076 (progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000076 "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), also known as progressive multifocal leukoencephalitis, is a rare and usually fatal viral disease that is characterized by progressive damage (-pathy) or inflammation of the white matter (leuko-) of the brain (-encephalo-) at multiple locations (multifocal).

It occurs almost exclusively in people with severe immune deficiency, such as transplant patients on immunosuppressive medications, patients receiving certain kinds of chemotherapy, patients receiving natalizumab (Tysabri) for multiple sclerosis, psoriasis patients on long-term efalizumab (Raptiva) or AIDS patients.

It is caused by a virus, the JC virus, which is normally present and kept under control by the immune system. Immunosuppressive drugs prevent the immune system from controlling the virus.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000076 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000076 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_multifocal_leukoencephalopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000076 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12551")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000076 "See the comment for 'demyelinating autoimmune disease'.  Perhaps this class should be subsumed under the class 'demyelinating infectious disease'.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000076 "See the following article for evidence supporting making 'progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy' a subclass of 'infectious demyelinating disease'.

Robert Weissert
\"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy\"
Journal of Neuroimmunology
Volume 231, Issues 1-2, February 2011, Pages 73-77
Special Issue on Advances in Clinical Neuroimmunology ")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000076 "progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000076 obo:ND_0000155)

# Class: obo:ND_0000077 (subacute combined degeneration)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000077 "Subacute combined degeneration is a disorder of the spine, brain, and nerves that involves weakness, abnormal sensations, mental problems, and vision difficulties.

Causes, incidence, and risk factors

Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord is caused by a vitamin B12 deficiency. (For specific information on vitamin B12 deficiency, see the article on pernicious anemia.)

Subacute combined degeneration primarily affects the spinal cord but its effects on the brain and the peripheral (body) nerves are the reason for the term “combined”. At first, the disease damages the covering of the nerves (the myelin sheath). It later affects the entire nerve cell.

How a lack of vitamin B12 damages nerves is unclear. However, experts believe the lack of this vitamin causes abnormal fatty acids to form around cells and nerves.

You have a higher risk for this condition if you cannot absorb vitamin B12 from the intestines or if you have:
    Pernicious anemia
    Disorders of the small intestine, including Crohn's disease
    Malabsorptive conditions, which can occur after gastrointestinal surgery

Symptoms include:
    Abnormal sensations (tingling and numbness)
    Weakness of the legs, arms, or other areas
These symptoms slowly get worse and are usually felt on both sides of the body.

Other symptoms include:
    Clumsiness, stiff, or awkward movements
    Change in mental state such as memory problems, irritability, apathy, confusion, or dementia
    Decreased vision
    Depression
    Sleepiness
    Unsteady gait and loss of balance")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000077 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000077 "SCD")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000077 "subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000077 "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmedhealth/PMH0001737/")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000077 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12564")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000077 "subacute combined degeneration")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000077 obo:ND_0000024)

# Class: obo:ND_0000078 (epilepsy disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:ND_0000078 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000078 "Epilepsy disease is a neurological disease where a greater than normal disposition to have seizures is present in an organism.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000078 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000078 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12718")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000078 "Epilepsies are classified in five ways:

    By their first cause (or etiology).
    By the observable manifestations of the seizures, known as semiology.
    By the location in the brain where the seizures originate.
    As a part of discrete, identifiable medical syndromes.
    By the event that triggers the seizures, such as reading or music

In 1981, the International League Against Epilepsy (ILAE) proposed a classification scheme for individual seizures that remains in common use. This classification is based on observation (clinical and EEG) rather than the underlying pathophysiology or anatomy and is outlined later on in this article. In 1989, the ILAE proposed a classification scheme for epilepsies and epileptic syndromes. This can be broadly described as a two-axis scheme having the cause on one axis and the extent of localization within the brain on the other. Since 1997, the ILAE have been working on a new scheme that has five axes: ictal phenomenon, (pertaining to an epileptic seizure), seizure type, syndrome, etiology, impairment.

From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epilepsy#Classification")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000078 "This class was just called 'epilepsy' in NIF-Dysfunction, but that term is being used to label the syndrome associated with epilepsy disease in ND.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000078 "epilepsy disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000078 obo:ND_0000001)

