# Class: obo:ND_0000090 (Landau-Kleffner syndrome)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000090 "A syndrome characterized by the onset of isolated language dysfunction in otherwise normal children (age of onset 4-7 years) and epileptiform discharges on ELECTROENCEPHALOGRAPHY. Seizures, including atypical absence ( EPILEPSY, ABSENCE), complex partial ( EPILEPSY, COMPLEX PARTIAL), and other types may occur. The electroencephalographic abnormalities and seizures tend to resolve by puberty. The language disorder may also resolve although some individuals are left with severe language dysfunction, including APHASIA and auditory AGNOSIA.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000090 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000090 "Menkes, Textbook of Child Neurology, 5th ed, pp749-50; J Child Neurol 1997 Nov;12(8):489-495")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000090 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12743")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000090 "Landau-Kleffner syndrome")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000090 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000091 (myoclonic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000091 "A clinically diverse group of epilepsy syndromes characterized either by myoclonic seizures or by myoclonus in association with other seizure types. Myoclonic epilepsy syndromes are divided into three subtypes based on etiology: familial, cryptogenic, and symptomatic (i.e., occurring secondary to known disease processes such as infections, hypoxic-ischemic injuries, trauma, etc.).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000091 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000091 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Myoclonic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000091 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12719")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000091 "myoclonic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000091 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000092 (infantile myoclonic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000092 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000092 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12720")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000092 "infantile myoclonic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000092 obo:ND_0000091)

# Class: obo:ND_0000093 (juvenile myoclonic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000093 "A disorder characterized by the onset of myoclonus in adolescence, a marked increase in the incidence of absence seizures (see EPILEPSY, ABSENCE), and generalized major motor seizures (see EPILEPSY, TONIC-CLONIC). The myoclonic episodes tend to occur shortly after awakening. Seizures tend to be aggravated by sleep deprivation and alcohol consumption. Hereditary and sporadic forms have been identified.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000093 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000093 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Juvenile_Myoclonic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000093 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12721")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000093 "juvenile myoclonic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000093 obo:ND_0000091)

# Class: obo:ND_0000094 (progressive myoclonic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000094 "A heterogeneous group of primarily familial disorders characterized by myoclonic seizures, tonic-clonic seizures, ataxia, progressive intellectual deterioration, and neuronal degeneration. These include LAFORA DISEASE; MERRF SYNDROME; NEURONAL CEROID-LIPOFUSCINOSIS; sialidosis (see MUCOLIPIDOSES), and UNVERRICHT-LUNDBORG SYNDROME.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000094 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000094 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Progressive_Myoclonic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000094 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12722")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000094 "progressive myoclonic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000094 obo:ND_0000091)

# Class: obo:ND_0000095 (Lafora progressive myoclonic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000095 "A form of stimulus sensitive myoclonic epilepsy inherited as an autosomal recessive condition. The most common presenting feature is a single seizure in the second decade of life. This is followed by progressive myoclonus, myoclonic seizures, tonic-clonic seizures, focal occipital seizures, intellectual decline, and severe motor and coordination impairments. Most affected individuals do not live past the age of 25 years. Concentric amyloid (Lafora) bodies are found in neurons, liver, skin, bone, and muscle.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000095 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000095 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Lafora_Progressive_Myoclonic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000095 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12723")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000095 "Lafora progressive myoclonic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000095 obo:ND_0000094)

# Class: obo:ND_0000096 (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000096 "A mitochondrial encephalomyopathy characterized clinically by a mixed seizure disorder, myoclonus, progressive ataxia, spasticity, and a mild myopathy. Dysarthria, optic atrophy, growth retardation, deafness, and dementia may also occur. This condition tends to present in childhood and to be transmitted via maternal lineage. Muscle biopsies reveal ragged-red fibers and respiratory chain enzymatic defects.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000096 "MERRF syndrome (or Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers) is a mitochondrial disease. It is extremely rare, and has varying degrees of expressivity owing to heteroplasmy.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000096 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000096 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MERRF_syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000096 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:MERRF_Syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000096 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12724")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:ND_0000096 "MERRF syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:ND_0000096 "Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000096 "myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000096 obo:ND_0000094)

# Class: obo:ND_0000097 (Unverricht-Lundborg syndrome)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000097 "An autosomal recessive condition characterized by recurrent myoclonic and generalized seizures, ATAXIA, slowly progressive intellectual deterioration, dysarthria, and intention tremor. Myoclonic seizures are severe and continuous, and tend to be triggered by movement, stress, and sensory stimuli. The age of onset is between 8 and 13 years, and the condition is relatively frequent in the Baltic region, especially Finland.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000097 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000097 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Unverricht-Lundborg_Syndrome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000097 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12725")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000097 "Unverricht-Lundborg syndrome")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000097 obo:ND_0000094)

# Class: obo:ND_0000098 (partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000098 "Conditions characterized by recurrent paroxysmal neuronal discharges which arise from a focal region of the brain. Partial seizures are divided into simple and complex, depending on whether consciousness is unaltered (simple partial seizure) or disturbed (complex partial seizure). Both types may feature a wide variety of motor, sensory, and autonomic symptoms. Partial seizures may be classified by associated clinical features or anatomic location of the seizure focus. A secondary generalized seizure refers to a partial seizure that spreads to involve the brain diffusely.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000098 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000098 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Partial_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000098 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12726")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000098 "partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000098 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000099 (complex partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000099 "A disorder characterized by recurrent partial seizures marked by impairment of cognition. During the seizure the individual may experience a wide variety of psychic phenomenon including formed hallucinations, illusions, deja vu, intense emotional feelings, confusion, and spatial disorientation. Focal motor activity, sensory alterations and AUTOMATISM may also occur. Complex partial seizures often originate from foci in one or both temporal lobes. The etiology may be idiopathic (cryptogenic partial complex epilepsy) or occur as a secondary manifestation of a focal cortical lesion (symptomatic partial complex epilepsy).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000099 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000099 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Complex_Partial_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000099 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12728")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000099 "complex partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000099 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000100 (frontal lobe epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000100 "A localization-related (focal) form of epilepsy characterized by seizures which arise in the frontal lobe. A variety of clinical syndromes exist depending on the exact location of the seizure focus. Simple or complex motor movements may occur, and most commonly involve the face and upper extremities. Seizures in the anterior frontal regions may be associated with head and eye turning, typically away from the side of origin of the seizure. Frontal lobe seizures may be idiopathic (cryptogenic) or caused by an identifiable disease process such as traumatic injuries, neoplasms, or other macroscopic or microscopic lesions of the frontal lobes (symptomatic frontal lobe seizures).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000100 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000100 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Frontal_Lobe_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000100 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12729")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000100 "frontal lobe epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000100 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000101 (motor partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000101 "A disorder characterized by recurrent localized paroxysmal discharges of cerebral neurons that give rise to seizures that have motor manifestations. The majority of partial motor seizures originate in the FRONTAL LOBE (see also EPILEPSY, FRONTAL LOBE). Motor seizures may manifest as tonic or clonic movements involving the face, one limb or one side of the body. A variety of more complex patterns of movement, including abnormal posturing of extremities, may also occur.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000101 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000101 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Motor_Partial_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000101 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12730")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000101 "motor partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000101 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000102 (rolandic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000102 "An autosomal dominant inherited partial epilepsy syndrome with onset between age 3 and 13 years. Seizures are characterized by PARESTHESIA and tonic or clonic activity of the lower face associated with drooling and dysarthria. The episodes tend to occur at night and may become secondarily generalized. In most cases, affected children are neurologically and developmentally normal. The electroencephalogram shows characteristic high-voltage sharp waves over the central temporal regions, which are more prominent during drowsiness and sleep. In general, seizures do not continue beyond mid-adolescence.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000102 "In neurology and pediatrics, benign rolandic epilepsy or benign (childhood) epilepsy with centrotemporal (EEG) spikes (also known as sylvian seizures) is the most common epilepsy syndrome in childhood. Most children will outgrow the syndrome (it starts around the age of 3-13 with a peak around 8–9 years and stops around age 14-18), hence the label benign. The seizures start around the central sulcus of the brain (also called the centrotemporal area, located around the Rolandic fissure, after Luigi Rolando).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000102 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000102 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolandic_epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000102 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Rolandic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000102 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12732")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000102 "rolandic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000102 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000103 (sensory partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000103 "A disorder characterized by recurrent focal onset seizures which have sensory (i.e., olfactory, visual, tactile, gustatory, or auditory) manifestations. Partial seizures that feature alterations of consciousness are referred to as complex partial seizures ( EPILEPSY, COMPLEX PARTIAL).")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000103 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000103 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Sensory_Partial_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000103 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12731")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000103 "sensory partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000103 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000104 (simple partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000104 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000104 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12727")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000104 "simple partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000104 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000105 (temporal partial epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000105 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000105 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12733")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000105 "temporal partial epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000105 obo:ND_0000098)

# Class: obo:ND_0000106 (post traumatic epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000106 "Recurrent seizures causally related to CRANIOCEREBRAL TRAUMA. Seizure onset may be immediate but is typically delayed for several days after the injury and may not occur for up to two years. The majority of seizures have a focal onset that correlates clinically with the site of brain injury. Cerebral cortex injuries caused by a penetrating foreign object ( CRANIOCEREBRAL TRAUMA, PENETRATING) are more likely than closed head injuries ( HEAD INJURIES, CLOSED) to be associated with epilepsy. Concussive convulsions are nonepileptic phenomena that occur immediately after head injury and are characterized by tonic and clonic movements.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000106 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000106 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Post-Traumatic_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000106 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12745")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000106 "post traumatic epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000106 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000107 (reflex epilepsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000107 "A subtype of epilepsy characterized by seizures that are consistently provoked by a certain specific stimulus. Auditory, visual, and somatosensory stimuli as well as the acts of writing, reading, eating, and decision making are examples of events or activities that may induce seizure activity in affected individuals.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000107 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000107 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Reflex_Epilepsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000107 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12746")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000107 "reflex epilepsy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000107 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000108 (seizure)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:ND_0000108 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000108 "Clinical or subclinical disturbances of cortical function due to a sudden, abnormal, excessive, and disorganized discharge of brain cells. Clinical manifestations include abnormal motor, sensory and psychic phenomena. Recurrent seizures are usually referred to as EPILEPSY or seizure disorder.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000108 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000108 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Seizures")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000108 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12748")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000108 "seizure")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000108 obo:OGMS_0000061)

# Class: obo:ND_0000109 (status epilepticus)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000109 "A prolonged seizure or seizures repeated frequently enough to prevent recovery between episodes occurring over a period of 20-30 minutes. The most common subtype is generalized tonic-clonic status epilepticus, a potentially fatal condition associated with neuronal injury and respiratory and metabolic dysfunction. Nonconvulsive forms include petit mal status and complex partial status, which may manifest as behavioral disturbances. Simple partial status epilepticus consists of persistent motor, sensory, or autonomic seizures that do not impair cognition (see also EPILEPSIA PARTIALIS CONTINUA). Subclinical status epilepticus generally refers to seizures occurring in an unresponsive or comatose individual in the absence of overt signs of seizure activity.
")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000109 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000109 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Status_Epilepticus")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000109 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12750")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000109 "Make this a syndrome only.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000109 "status epilepticus")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000109 obo:ND_0000078)

# Class: obo:ND_0000110 (epilepsia partialis continua)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000110 "A variant of epilepsy characterized by continuous focal jerking of a body part over a period of hours, days, or even years without spreading to other body regions. Contractions may be aggravated by movement and are reduced, but not abolished during sleep. ELECTROENCEPHALOGRAPHY demonstrates epileptiform (spike and wave) discharges over the hemisphere opposite to the affected limb in most instances. The repetitive movements may originate from the CEREBRAL CORTEX or from subcortical structures (e.g., BRAIN STEM; BASAL GANGLIA). This condition is associated with Russian Spring and Summer encephalitis (see ENCEPHALITIS, TICK BORNE); Rasmussen syndrome (see ENCEPHALITIS); MULTIPLE SCLEROSIS; DIABETES MELLITUS; BRAIN NEOPLASMS; and CEREBROVASCULAR DISORDERS.
")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000110 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000110 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Epilepsia_Partialis_Continua")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000110 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12751")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000110 "epilepsia partialis continua")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000110 obo:ND_0000109)

# Class: obo:ND_0000111 (metabolic nervous system disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000111 "Brain dysfunction or damage caused by acquired (i.e., non-inborn) metabolic disorders. Associated conditions include ENDOCRINE DISEASES; WATER-ELECTROLYTE IMBALANCE; KIDNEY DISEASES; LIVER DISEASES; anoxia ( HYPOXIA, BRAIN); nutritional disorders (see NUTRITIONAL AND METABOLIC DISEASES); an encephalopathy associated with HEMODIALYSIS; and other disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000111 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000111 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Metabolic_Nervous_System_Disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000111 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12705")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000111 "metabolic nervous system disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000111 obo:ND_0000001)

# Class: obo:ND_0000112 (inborn metabolic brain disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000112 "Brain disorders resulting from inborn metabolic errors, primarily from enzymatic defects which lead to substrate accumulation, product reduction, or increase in toxic metabolites through alternate pathways. The majority of these conditions are familial, however spontaneous mutation may also occur in utero.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000112 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000112 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Inborn_Metabolic_Brain_Disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000112 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12706")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000112 "inborn metabolic brain disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000112 obo:ND_0000111)

# Class: obo:ND_0000113 (neurodegenerative disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000113 "A neurological disease that is characterized by atrophy or death of neurons or related structures progressively affecting the functioning of the nervous system.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000113 "Hereditary and sporadic conditions which are characterized by progressive nervous system dysfunction. These disorders are often associated with atrophy of the affected central or peripheral nervous system structures.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000113 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000113 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Neurodegenerative_disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000113 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_2086")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000113 "neurodegenerative disease")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000113 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000001 ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006042)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000113 obo:ND_0000001)

# Class: obo:ND_0000114 (Huntingtons disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000114 "A familial disorder inherited as an autosomal dominant trait and characterized by the onset of progressive CHOREA and DEMENTIA in the fourth or fifth decade of life. Common initial manifestations include paranoia; poor impulse control; DEPRESSION; HALLUCINATIONS; and DELUSIONS. Eventually intellectual impairment; loss of fine motor control; ATHETOSIS; and diffuse chorea involving axial and limb musculature develops, leading to a vegetative state within 10-15 years of disease onset. The juvenile variant has a more fulminant course including SEIZURES; ATAXIA; dementia; and chorea.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000114 "Huntington's disease is an inherited disease that causes the progressive breakdown (degeneration) of nerve cells in the brain. Huntington's disease has a broad impact on a person's functional abilities and usually results in movement, thinking (cognitive) and psychiatric disorders.
")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000114 "Huntington's disease, chorea, or disorder (HD), is a neurodegenerative genetic disorder that affects muscle coordination and leads to cognitive decline and dementia. It typically becomes noticeable in middle age. HD is the most common genetic cause of abnormal involuntary writhing movements called chorea. It is much more common in people of Western European descent than in those of Asian or African ancestry. The disease is caused by an autosomal dominant mutation on either of an individual's two copies of a gene called Huntingtin, which means any child of an affected parent has a 50% risk of inheriting the disease. In the rare situations where both parents have an affected copy, the risk increases to 75%, and when either parent has two affected copies, the risk is 100% (all children will be affected). Physical symptoms of Huntington's disease can begin at any age from infancy to old age, but usually begin between 35 and 44 years of age. About 6% of cases start before the age of 21 years with an akinetic-rigid syndrome; they progress faster and vary slightly. The variant is classified as juvenile, akinetic-rigid or Westphal variant HD.

Symptoms of the disease can vary between individuals and among affected members of the same family, but the symptoms progress predictably for most individuals. The earliest symptoms are a general lack of coordination and an unsteady gait. As the disease advances, uncoordinated, jerky body movements become more apparent, along with a decline in mental abilities and behavioral and psychiatric problems. Physical abilities are gradually impeded until coordinated movement becomes very difficult. Mental abilities generally decline into dementia.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000114 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000114 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huntington%27s_disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000114 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Huntingtons_disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000114 "http://www.mayoclinic.com/health/huntingtons-disease/DS00401")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000114 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12500")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000114 "Consider adding a class of diseases named 'polyglutamine disease' and make Huntingtons disease a child of polyglutamine disease.  Also add the classes 'dentato-rubral and pallido-luysian atrophy' (DRPLA) and 'spino-cerebellar ataxia' (SCA) and make them children of polyglutamine disease.

From the paper, \"Protein aggregation and neurodegenerative disease\"")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000114 "Huntingtons disease")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000114 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000113 ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006041)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000114 obo:ND_0000113)

# Class: obo:ND_0000115 (akinetic rgid variant of Huntingtons disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000115 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000115 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12504")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000115 "akinetic rgid variant of Huntingtons disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000115 obo:ND_0000114)

# Class: obo:ND_0000116 (juvenile onset Huntingtons disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000116 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000116 "juvenile-onset Huntingtons disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000116 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12505")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000116 "Are late-onset and juvenile-onset two different diseases or should they be eliminated?")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000116 "juvenile onset Huntingtons disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000116 obo:ND_0000114)

# Class: obo:ND_0000117 (late onset Huntingtons disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000117 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000117 "late-onset Huntingtons disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000117 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12506")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000117 "Are late-onset and juvenile-onset two different diseases or should they be eliminated?")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000117 "late onset Huntingtons disease")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000117 obo:ND_0000114)

# Class: obo:ND_0000118 (Lewy body disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000118 "A neurodegenerative disease characterized by dementia, mild parkinsonism, and fluctuations in attention and alertness. The neuropsychiatric manifestations tend to precede the onset of bradykinesia, MUSCLE RIGIDITY, and other extrapyramidal signs. DELUSIONS and visual HALLUCINATIONS are relatively frequent in this condition. Histologic examination reveals LEWY BODIES in the CEREBRAL CORTEX and BRAIN STEM. SENILE PLAQUES and other pathologic features characteristic of ALZHEIMER DISEASE may also be present.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000118 "Lewy body disease is a neurological disease where cognitive deficits [memory, visual spatial, attention, orientation, executive function, functional] occur that represent a decline from previous levels of functioning resulting in dementia as well as visual hallucinations or fluctuations or REM sleep behavior disorder or Parkonsinism occur caused by neurodegeneration as a result of deposition of Lewy bodies in the cerebral cortex and limbic structures.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000118 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "DLB")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "LBD")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "Lewy body dementia")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "cortical Lewy body disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "dementia with Lewy bodies")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "diffuse Lewy body disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000118 "senile dementia of Lewy type")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000118 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Lewy_Body_Disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000118 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12502")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000118 "Lewy body disease")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000118 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000068 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000113 obo:ND_0005505) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000113 obo:ND_0005508) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:ND_0004000 obo:ND_0003000) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:ND_0004000 obo:ND_0003003) ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006038)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000118 obo:ND_0000068)

# Class: obo:ND_0000119 (motor neuron disease)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000119 "Diseases characterized by a selective degeneration of the motor neurons of the spinal cord, brainstem, or motor cortex. Clinical subtypes are distinguished by the major site of degeneration. In AMYOTROPHIC LATERAL SCLEROSIS there is involvement of upper, lower, and brainstem motor neurons. In progressive muscular atrophy and related syndromes (see MUSCULAR ATROPHY, SPINAL) the motor neurons in the spinal cord are primarily affected. With progressive bulbar palsy ( BULBAR PALSY, PROGRESSIVE), the initial degeneration occurs in the brainstem. In primary lateral sclerosis, the cortical neurons are affected in isolation.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000119 "The motor neuron diseases (or motor neuron diseases) (MND) are a group of neurological disorders that selectively affect motor neurons, the cells that control voluntary muscle activity including speaking, walking, breathing, swallowing and general movement of the body.

Forms of motor neuron disease include:
    amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) (sometimes called Lou Gehrig's disease)
    primary lateral sclerosis (PLS)
    progressive muscular atrophy (PMA)
    bulbar
        pseudobulbar palsy - spastic
        progressive bulbar palsy - spastic and flaccid
")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000119 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000119 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motor_neurone_disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000119 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Motor_Neuron_Disease")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000119 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12565")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000119 "motor neuron disease")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000119 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000113 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:ND_0004000 obo:ND_0003001) ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006032)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000119 obo:ND_0000113)

# Class: obo:ND_0000120 (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000120 "A degenerative disorder affecting upper MOTOR NEURONS in the brain and lower motor neurons in the brain stem and SPINAL CORD. Disease onset is usually after the age of 50 and the process is usually fatal within 3 to 6 years. Clinical manifestations include progressive weakness, atrophy, FASCICULATION, hyperreflexia, DYSARTHRIA, dysphagia, and eventual paralysis of respiratory function. Pathologic features include the replacement of motor neurons with fibrous ASTROCYTES and atrophy of anterior SPINAL NERVE ROOTS and corticospinal tracts.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000120 "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also referred to as Lou Gehrig's disease, is a form of motor neuron disease caused by the degeneration of neurons located in the ventral horn of the spinal cord and the cortical neurons that provide their afferent input. The condition is often called Lou Gehrig's disease in North America, after the famous New York Yankees baseball player who was diagnosed with the disease in 1939. The disorder is characterized by rapidly progressive weakness, muscle atrophy and fasciculations, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and respiratory compromise. Sensory function generally is spared, as is autonomic, and oculomotor activity. ALS is a progressive, fatal, neurodegenerative disease with most affected patients dying of respiratory compromise and pneumonia after 2 to 3 years; although some perish within a year from the onset of symptoms, and occasional individuals have a more indolent course and survive for many years.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000120 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000120 "ALS")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000120 "Lou Gehrig's disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000120 "Lou Gehrigs disease")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000120 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000120 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Amyotrophic_Lateral_Sclerosis")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0000120 "See the following link for information on subtypes/variants of ALS:http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php
Also see the following for an alternative terms:
http://www.alsa.org/about-als/forms-of-als.html

-COX
11/21/13")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000120 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12566")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000120 "amyotrophic lateral sclerosis")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000120 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000119 ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006033)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000120 obo:ND_0000119)

# Class: obo:ND_0000121 (progressive bulbar palsy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000121 "A motor neuron disease marked by progressive weakness of the muscles innervated by cranial nerves of the lower brain stem. Clinical manifestations include dysarthria, dysphagia, facial weakness, tongue weakness, and fasciculations of the tongue and facial muscles. The adult form of the disease is marked initially by bulbar weakness which progresses to involve motor neurons throughout the neuroaxis. Eventually this condition may become indistinguishable from AMYOTROPHIC LATERAL SCLEROSIS. Fazio-Londe syndrome is an inherited form of this illness which occurs in children and young adults.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000121 "Progressive bulbar palsy (also known simply as PBP) is a medical condition. It belongs to a group of disorders known as motor neuron diseases (Lapiedra 2002). PBP is a disease that attacks the nerves supplying the bulbar muscles. These disorders are characterized by the degeneration of motor neurons in the cerebral cortex, spinal cord, brain stem, and pyramidal tracts. This specifically involves the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), vagus nerve (X), and hypoglossal nerve (XII) (Hughes 1998).

This disorder should not be confused with pseudobulbar palsy or progressive spinal muscular atrophy (Hughes 1998). The term Infantile progressive bulbar palsy is used to describe progressive bulbar palsy in children.

Some neurologists consider this disorder to be a subset of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), but others disagree with that classification.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000121 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000121 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_bulbar_palsy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000121 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Progressive_Bulbar_Palsy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000121 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12567")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000121 "progressive bulbar palsy")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000121 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000119 ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006034)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000121 obo:ND_0000119)

# Class: obo:ND_0000122 (spinal muscular atrophy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000122 "A group of recessively inherited diseases that feature progressive muscular atrophy and hypotonia. They are classified as type I (Werdnig-Hoffman disease), type II (intermediate form), and type III (Kugelberg-Welander disease). Type I is fatal in infancy, type II has a late infantile onset and is associated with survival into the second or third decade. Type III has its onset in childhood, and is slowly progressive.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000122 "Spinal Muscular Atrophy (SMA) is a neuromuscular disease characterized by degeneration of motor neurons, resulting in progressive muscular atrophy (wasting away) and weakness. The clinical spectrum of SMA ranges from early infant death to normal adult life with only mild weakness. These patients often require comprehensive medical care involving multiple disciplines, including pediatric pulmonology, pediatric neurology, pediatric orthopedic surgery, Lower Extremity & Spinal Orthosis, pediatric critical care, and physical medicine and rehabilitation; and physical therapy, occupational therapy, respiratory therapy, and clinical nutrition. Genetic counseling is also helpful for the parents and family members. Sensation and the ability to feel are not affected. Intellectual activity is normal and it is often observed that patients with SMA are unusually bright and sociable.
The term \"juvenile spinal muscular atrophy\" refers to Kugelberg-Welander syndrome.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000122 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000122 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinal_muscular_atrophy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000122 "http://neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Spinal_Muscular_Atrophy_(SMA)")
