# Class: obo:ND_0000186 (amyloidopathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000186 "Amyloidopathy is a neurological disease that results in the degeneration of populations of nerve cells due to the formation of extracellular amyloid plaques.

*** NEEDS REVIEW ***")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000186 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0000186 "Is this term unique to neurological diseases?  According to the following wikipedia entry, amyloidosis can occur in other organs such as the heart.  Does this pose a problem for our classification schema?

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amyloidosis

\"In medicine, amyloidosis refers to a variety of conditions wherein amyloid proteins are abnormally deposited in organs or tissues and cause harm. A protein is described as being amyloid if, due to an alteration in its secondary structure, it takes on a particular aggregated insoluble form, similar to the beta-pleated sheet. Symptoms vary widely depending upon where in the body amyloid deposits accumulate. Amyloidosis may be inherited or acquired.\"

UPDATE [01/14/2014]: Discussions about this issue have led to the proposal that 'amyloidopathy' be retained as a neurological disease specific term while 'amyloidosis' (which amyloidopathy is a subclass of) should be used as a general term for diseases that involve abnormal deposition of amyloid in any region of the body. A compelling reason for this decision is that the term 'amyloid' is historically applied loosely in the medical literature. For example, at least 24 proteins have been identified as constituents in amyloids and, in most cases, amyloid is not the primary protein constituent.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000186 "amyloidopathy")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000186 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000113 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000113 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0005504 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000082 obo:FMA_50801)))))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000186 obo:ND_0000113)

# Class: obo:ND_0000187 (FTD caused by CHMP2B mutations)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000187 "Early personality change is the most common feature which can include less concern for others, an unkempt appearance, disinhibition, inappropriate emotional responses and restlessness which later can be accompanied by aggression. Apathy can also occur as the disease progresses. Hyperorality is also common, encompassing mouthing of non-food objects, over-eating of sweet foods and chain smoking. A progressive aphasia develops during the course of the disease, although normally not with features of PNFA or SD. It is characterised by reduced spontaneous speech with relative preservation of reading and repetition, most consistent with a dynamic aphasia. An overt motor syndrome develops late in the disease course which can include parkinsonian features, dystonia, pyramidal signs and myoclonus, ultimately leaving the patients bedridden. None of the patients have had any clinical signs of either upper or lower motor neuron impairment, and no neurophysiological EMG studies have been done so far in the Danish FTD-3 patients. A new branch of the FTD-3 family was recently identified and clinical descriptions of a further two cases were similar to those previously reported [21]. The average age of onset for FTD-3 is 58 years of age and the mean duration is 10 years. Ongoing neuropsychological studies indicate that some of the patients can have impaired memory function in the earlier phases of the disease, but this is not a prominent feature and most often not a subjective complaint by the patients.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000187 "FTD caused by CHMP2B mutations is frontotemporal dementia that is caused by CHMP2B mutations.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000187 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000187 "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3182073/?tool=pubmed")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000187 "FTD caused by CHMP2B mutations")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000187 obo:ND_0000166)

# Class: obo:ND_0000188 (cerebral amyloid angiopathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000188 "A heterogeneous group of sporadic or familial disorders characterized by AMYLOID deposits in the walls of small and medium sized blood vessels of CEREBRAL CORTEX and MENINGES. Clinical features include multiple, small lobar CEREBRAL HEMORRHAGE; cerebral ischemia ( BRAIN ISCHEMIA); and CEREBRAL INFARCTION. Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is unrelated to generalizedAMYLOIDOSIS. Amyloidogenic peptides in this condition are nearly always the same ones found in ALZHEIMER DISEASE.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000188 "Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA), also known as congophilic angiopathy, is a form of angiopathy in which amyloid deposits form in the walls of the blood vessels of the central nervous system.  The amyloid material is only found in the brain and as such the disease is not related to other forms of amyloidosis.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000188 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000188 "CAA")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000188 "amyloid angiopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000188 "congophilic angiopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000188 "Kumar: Robbins and Cotran: Pathologic Basis of Disease, 7th ed., 2005")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000188 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebral_amyloid_angiopathy")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000188 "cerebral amyloid angiopathy")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0000188 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0000186 ObjectAllValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000054 obo:ND_0006036)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000188 obo:ND_0000186)

# Class: obo:ND_0000189 (chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0000189 "Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) is an acquired immune-mediated inflammatory disorder of the peripheral nervous system. The disorder is sometimes called chronic relapsing polyneuropathy. CIDP is closely related to Guillain-Barré syndrome and it is considered the chronic counterpart of that acute disease. Its symptoms are also similar to progressive inflammatory neuropathy. An asymmetrical variant of CIDP is known as Lewis-Sumner syndrome.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000189 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0000189 "Mark Jensen")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000189 "CIDP")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000189 "chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0000189 "chronic relapsing polyneuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0000189 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronic_inflammatory_demyelinating_polyneuropathy")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0000189 "Change entity URI")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0000189 "http://ontology.neuinfo.org/NIF/Dysfunction/NIF-Dysfunction.owl#birnlex_12556")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0000189 "An example of a demylinating disease that affects the PNS. MJ")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0000189 "chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0000189 obo:ND_0000067)

