# Class: obo:ND_00055012 (protein)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_00055012 "A protein is a biochemical compound consisting of one or more polypeptides typically folded into a globular or fibrous form, that facilitates a biological function.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_00055012 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_00055012 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protein")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_00055012 "We may or may not want to add more terms related to proteins such as:

A polypeptide is a single linear polymer chain of amino acids bonded together by peptide bonds between the carboxyl and amino groups of adjacent amino acid residues. The sequence of amino acids in a protein is defined by the sequence of a gene, which is encoded in the genetic code.

From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protein")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_00055012 "protein")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_00055012 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:BFO_0000040 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000110 obo:ND_0005033)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_00055012 obo:BFO_0000040)

# Class: obo:ND_0005502 (neurofibrillary tangle)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005502 "Neurofibrillary tangle is an intracellular aggregation of tau proteins that develop in neurons in the brain.  The development of neurofibrillary tangles typically results in the disruption of the transportation of nutrients and other important substances within the neuron, which typically results in the destruction and death of the cell.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005502 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005502 "NFT")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005502 "PHF")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005502 "paired helical filaments")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005502 "tangle")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005502 "tau tangle")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005502 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0005502 "Neurofibrillary Tangles (NFTs) are aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau protein that are most commonly known as a primary marker of Alzheimer's Disease. Their presence is also found in numerous other diseases known as Tauopathies. Little is known about their exact relationship to the different pathologies.

Neurofibrillary tangles are formed by hyperphosphorylation of a microtubule-associated protein known as tau, causing it to aggregate, or group, in an insoluble form. (These aggregations of hyperphosphorylated tau protein are also referred to as PHF, or \"paired helical filaments\"). The precise mechanism of tangle formation is not completely understood, and it is still controversial whether tangles are a primary causative factor in disease or play a more peripheral role.

From:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurofibrillary_tangles")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005502 "neurofibrillary tangle")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0005502 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0005511 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000110 obo:ND_0005019)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005502 obo:ND_0005511)

# Class: obo:ND_0005503 (autoimmune demyelinating disorder)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:ND_0005503 obo:IAO_0000123)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005503 "A disorder that bears the disposition for an autoimmune demyelinating disease.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000116 obo:ND_0005503 "This may not be correct. It is possible that once we have the material bases for these diseases worked out, there may be no unifying property and the fact they are all neurolgical disorders will be inferred. But, for now, this class provides readability, organization and reasoning, etc.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005503 "Mark Jensen")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005503 "TO DO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005503 "autoimmune demyelinating disorder")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005503 obo:ND_0005501)
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005503 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000127 obo:ND_0000026))

# Class: obo:ND_0005504 (amyloid aggregate)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005504 "An amyloid aggregate is a typically extracellular aggregation of amyloid fibrils.

TO DO: Revise")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005504 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005504 "amyloid")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0005504 "Pathogenesis of amyloidosis:

When a native cell creates a protein, it could either make the actual protein or protein fragments. These fragments could come and join together to form the actual protein. Such a protein can sometimes regress into the protein fragments. This process of \"flip flopping\" happens frequently in certain proteins, especially the ones that cause this disease.

The fragments or actual proteins are at risk of mis-folding as they are synthesized, to make a bad protein. This causes proteolysis, which is the directed degradation of proteins by cellular enzymes called proteases or by intramolecular digestion; proteases come and digest the mis-folded fragments and proteins. The problem occurs when the proteins do not dissolve in proteolysis. This happens because the mis-folded proteins sometimes become robust enough that they are not dissolved by normal proteolysis. When the fragments do not dissolve, they get spit out of proteolysis and they aggregate to form oligomers. The reason they aggregate is that the parts of the protein that do not dissolve in proteolysis are the β-pleated sheets, which are extremely hydrophobic. They are usually sequestered in the middle of the protein, while parts of the protein that are more soluble are found near the outside. When they are exposed to water, these hydrophobic pieces tend to aggregate with other hydrophobic pieces. This ball of fragments gets stabilized by GAG's (glycosaminoglycans) and SAP (serum amyloid P), a component found in amyloid aggregations that is thought to stabilize them and prevent proteolytic cleavage). The stabilized balls of protein fragments are called oligomers. The oligomers can aggregate together and further stabilize to make amyloid fibrils.

Both the oligomers and amyloid fibrils can cause cell toxicity and organ dysfunction.

From:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amyloidosis

Also see:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amyloid")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005504 "amyloid aggregate")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005504 obo:ND_0005511)

# Class: obo:ND_0005505 (Lewy body)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005505 "Lewy body is an abnormal aggregate of protein that develops inside nerve cells in Parkinson's disease (PD), Lewy Body Dementia and some other disorders. They are identified under the microscope when histology is performed on the brain.  Lewy bodies appear as spherical masses that displace other cell components.  There are two morphological types: classical (brain stem) Lewy bodies and cortical Lewy bodies.

A Lewy body is composed of the protein alpha-synuclein associated with other proteins such as ubiquitin, neurofilament protein, and alpha B crystallin.  Tau proteins may also be present, and Lewy bodies may occasionally be surrounded by neurofibrillary tangles.

It is believed that Lewy bodies represent an aggresome response in the cell.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005505 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005505 "WEB:http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewy_body@2012-08-15")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005505 "Lewy body")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0005505 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0005509 ObjectUnionOf(ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005019) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005026) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005029) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005032)) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005023)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005505 obo:ND_0005509)

# Class: obo:ND_0005506 (classical Lewy body)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005506 "A classical Lewy body is an eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion that consists of a dense core surrounded by a halo of 10-nm wide radiating fibrils, the primary structural component of which is alpha-synuclein.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005506 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005506 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewy_body")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:synonym obo:ND_0005506 "brain stem Lewy body")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005506 "classical Lewy body")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005506 obo:ND_0005505)

# Class: obo:ND_0005507 (cortical Lewy body)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005507 "A cortical Lewy body is an eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion that is composed of alpha-synuclein fibrils, but is less well defined than a classical Lewy body and lacks the halo.  Cortical Lewy bodies are a distinguishing feature of Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) and may occasionally be seen in ballooned neurons characteristic of Pick's disease and corticobasal degeneration, as well as in patients with other tauopathies.  They are also seen in cases of multiple system atrophy, particularly the Parkinsonian variant.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005507 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005507 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewy_body")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005507 "cortical Lewy body")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005507 obo:ND_0005505)

# Class: obo:ND_0005508 (Lewy neurite)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005508 "Similar to Lewy bodies, Lewy neurites are proteinaceous formations found in neurons of the disease brain, comprising abnormal α-synuclein filaments and granular material.  They are a feature of α-synucleinopathies such as Dementia with Lewy bodies, Parkinson's disease and multiple system atrophy (MSA), and are found in the CA2-3 region of the hippocampus in Alzheimer's disease.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005508 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005508 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewy_body")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005508 "Lewy neurite")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005508 obo:ND_0005509)

