AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005509 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0005509 "filamentous inclusion made of the protein alpha-synuclein")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0005509 "This should probably be a subclass of 'amyloid aggregate'

-APC")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005509 "alpha synuclein aggregate")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0005509 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:ND_0005511 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000110 obo:ND_0005023)))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005509 obo:ND_0005511)

# Class: obo:ND_0005510 (aggresome)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:ND_0005510 obo:IAO_0000423)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005510 "An aggresome is a proteinaceous inclusion body that forms when cellular degradation machinery is impaired or overwhelmed, leading to an accumulation of protein for disposal. An aggresome is formed as the result of an aggresomal response.

Typically, an aggresome forms in response to a cellular stress which generates a large amount of misfolded or partially denatured protein: hyperthermia, overexpression of an insoluble or mutant protein, etc. The formation of the aggresome is largely believed to be a protective response, sequestering potentially cytotoxic aggregates and also acting as a staging center for eventual autophagic clearance from the cell.

An aggresome forms around the microtubule organizing center in eukaryotic cells, adjacent to or enveloping the cell's centrosomes. Lysine linked (63) polyubitiuntation tags the protein for retrograde transport via HDAC6 binding and microtubule-based motor protein, dynein. The protein aggregate is transported through the microtubule and unloaded via p97 forming the aggresome. Mediators such as p62 are believed to be involved in aggrosome formation in sequestering omega-somes, which bind and increase the size of the aggreseome. The aggreseome is eventually targeted for autophagic clearance for the cell. Some pathological proteins, such as a-synuclein, cannot be degraded and cause the aggresomes to form inclusion bodies or, in Parkinsons, Lewy bodies, which contribute to neuronal dysfunction and death.

Certain cellular inclusions seen in human disease are thought to represent an aggresomal response, such as the Lewy body seen in neurons in the brain in Parkinson's disease and Mallory's Hyaline seen in liver cells in conditions such as alcoholic liver disease.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005510 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005510 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aggresome

*** REVIEW THIS SOURCE FOR ACCURACY ***")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0005510 "TO DO: import this term from GO")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:ND_0005510 "http://amigo.geneontology.org/cgi-bin/amigo/term_details?term=GO:0016235")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005510 "aggresome")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005510 obo:BFO_0000040)

# Class: obo:ND_0005511 (protein aggregate)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:ND_0005511 obo:IAO_0000423)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0005511 "A protein aggregate is a grouping of misfolded proteins that is often rigid and insoluble.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000116 obo:ND_0005511 "I don't think it is correct to have this typed as a disorder. Are ALL protein aggregates the material basis of some disease?
-MJ")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000116 obo:ND_0005511 "This is a place holder until PR takes over this term.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000116 obo:ND_0005511 "This term label assumes that all protein aggregates are pathological in nature.  A review of the literature supports this interpretation; however, it is still an open question wether there are some nonpathological protein aggregates.  If this is the case, then this lable should be changed to 'pathological protein aggregate' and another class should be created labled 'protein aggregate' that is not a subclass of 'disorder'.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0005511 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0005511 "http://groups.molbiosci.northwestern.edu/holmgren/Glossary/Definitions/Def-P/protein_aggregate.html")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0005511 "protein aggregate")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005511 obo:BFO_0000040)
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005511 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000127 obo:ND_0000152))
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0005511 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(<http://www.obofoundry.org/ro/ro.owl#has_part> obo:ND_0005018))

# Class: obo:ND_0006000 (neurodegeneration)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0006000 "Neurodegeneration is a pathological bodily process that produces the loss of structure or function of neurons and can also produce the death of neurons.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0006000 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0006000 "Source?")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0006000 "neurodegeneration")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0006000 obo:OGMS_0000061)

# Class: obo:ND_0006001 (demyelination)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000111 obo:ND_0006001 "demyelination")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0006001 "Demyelination is a pathological bodily process that produces damage to the myelin sheath of neurons.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0006001 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0006001 "demyelination")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0006001 obo:OGMS_0000061)

# Class: obo:ND_0006002 (aggresomal response)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0006002 "An aggresomal response is believed to be a generalised-protective cell biological response to the presence of a high load of abnormal or damaged protein within the cytosol of a cell which fails to be eliminated by the usual ubiquitin proteasome system for protein degradation.

Typically, an aggresome forms in response to a cellular stress which generates a large amount of misfolded or partially denatured protein: hyperthermia, overexpression of an insoluble or mutant protein, etc. The formation of the aggresome is largely believed to be a protective response, sequestering potentially cytotoxic aggregates and also acting as a staging center for eventual autophagic clearance from the cell.

An aggresome forms around the microtubule organizing center in eukaryotic cells, adjacent to or enveloping the cell's centrosomes. Lysine linked (63) polyubitiuntation tags the protein for retrograde transport via HDAC6 binding and microtubule-based motor protein, dynein. The protein aggregate is transported through the microtubule and unloaded via p97 forming the aggresome. Mediators such as p62 are believed to be involved in aggrosome formation in sequestering omega-somes, which bind and increase the size of the aggreseome. The aggreseome is eventually targeted for autophagic clearance for the cell. Some pathological proteins, such as a-synuclein, cannot be degraded and cause the aggresomes to form inclusion bodies or, in Parkinsons, Lewy bodies, which contribute to neuronal dysfunction and death.

Certain cellular inclusions seen in human disease are thought to represent an aggresomal response, such as the Lewy body seen in neurons in the brain in Parkinson's disease and Mallory's Hyaline seen in liver cells in conditions such as alcoholic liver disease.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0006002 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:ND_0006002 "aggresome response")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:ND_0006002 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aggresome

*** REVIEW THIS SOURCE FOR ACCURACY ***")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0006002 "Is it correct to give this a logical definition that includes:

'has specified output' some aggresome ???

In other words, does every aggresomal response generate an aggresome?

See also: 'protein aggregation'

Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0006002 "Intracellular deposition of misfolded protein aggregates into ubiquitin-rich cytoplasmic inclusions is linked to the pathogenesis of many diseases. Why these aggregates form despite the existence of cellular machinery to recognize and degrade misfolded protein and how they are delivered to cytoplasmic inclusions are not known. We have investigated the intracellular fate of cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR), an inefficiently folded integral membrane protein which is degraded by the cytoplasmic ubiquitin-proteasome pathway. Overexpression or inhibition of proteasome activity in transfected human embryonic kidney or Chinese hamster ovary cells led to the accumulation of stable, high molecular weight, detergent-insoluble, multiubiquitinated forms of CFTR. Using immunofluorescence and transmission electron microscopy with immunogold labeling, we demonstrate that undegraded CFTR molecules accumulate at a distinct pericentriolar structure which we have termed the aggresome.  Aggresome formation is accompanied by redistribution of the intermediate filament protein vimentin to form a cage surrounding a pericentriolar core of aggregated, ubiquitinated protein. Disruption of microtubules blocks the formation of aggresomes. Similarly, inhibition of proteasome function also prevented the degradation of unassembled presenilin-1 molecules leading to their aggregation and deposition in aggresomes. These data lead us to propose that aggresome formation is a general response of cells which occurs when the capacity of the proteasome is exceeded by the production of aggregation-prone misfolded proteins.

Source: http://jcb.rupress.org/content/143/7/1883.abstract")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0006002 "WARNING: Not all aggresomal responses are pathological!  This term should be labled 'pathological aggresomal response' instead and a new class labled 'aggresomal response' should be created as a bodily process.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0006002 "aggresome assembly

From: http://amigo.geneontology.org/cgi-bin/amigo/term_details?term=GO:0070842

*** MAKE THIS ANOTHER BODILY PROCESS AND THEN ADD 'aggresomal response' has_part 'aggresome assembly' ***")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0006002 "aggresomal response")
SubClassOf(obo:ND_0006002 obo:OGMS_0000060)

# Class: obo:ND_0006003 (protein aggregation)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:ND_0006003 "Protein aggregation is the process through which misfolded protein fragments group together to form a protein aggregate.")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000117 obo:ND_0006003 "Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000232 obo:ND_0006003 "Are 'pathological aggresomal response' and 'protein aggregation' the same process?  If not, what differentiates them?  Is 'protein aggregation' a subclass of 'pathological aggresomal response' as I have currently asserted?  If so, what other types of 'pathological aggresomal response' are there?  If not, why not?


Is it correct to give this a logical definition that includes:
'has specified output' some aggresome ???
In other words, does every aggresomal response generate an aggresome?

See also: 'aggresomal response'

Alexander P. Cox")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ND_0006003 "Consider adding subclasses of 'protein aggregate' to represent the following properties that they can have:

Aggregates of proteins may arise from several mechanisms and may be classified in numerous ways, including soluble/insoluble, covalent/noncovalent, reversible/irreversible, and native/denatured.

Source: http://www.aapsj.org/view.asp?art=aapsj080366")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ND_0006003 "protein aggregation")
EquivalentClasses(obo:ND_0006003 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:OGMS_0000061 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000057 obo:ND_0005018)))

